 "printed_page": null,
 "printed_page_note": "no printed page number is on this exposure. The `No.` of the printed heading and the figure after it stand at the top right of the leaf, where this exposure runs into the gutter and the paper goes black; nothing legible survives there. Recorded as unknown rather than inferred from the sorted filename order, which is not the book order.",
 "sheet_side": "right",
 "division": null,
 "division_note": "the printed heading reads `by the Marshal of the ____ District (or Territory) of` with `Illinois` written into the blank in the enumerator's hand and the district blank left unfilled. No enumerator's name is written on the exposure, so no division is recorded; 33S7-9YYJ-5D carries `S. W. Sherman` and this sheet does not, and it is not carried over from a neighbouring image.",
 "state": "Illinois",
 "county": "Cook",
 "reading": "scan_verified",
 "read_pass": "continuation cells",
 "lines_ruled_with_an_entry": 30,
 "line_count_note": "coverage.json inventoried this image at 31 lines `to the nearest line`. Read line by line it carries 30 entries, on a grid of pitch 76.0 px with its first line at y669 and its thirtieth at y2873; the body's closing rule stands at y2966, which leaves room for a thirty-first line at y2949 that is blank in every one of the sheet's 37 columns. The grid was fitted to the industry columns' own ink — the columns that close against their printed footings — and the TOTAL column was then read against it rather than used to build it, so the row index and the numbers are independent. `tools/fit_census_line_grid.py` on the 18 industry anchors alone answers pitch 76.25 with rms 3.56 and puts the last of them on line 29, against rms 17.3 for the next-best line count and 18.1 for the one below — a factor of five either way. Line 30 carries a TOTAL and nothing else, which is why the industry fit stops at 29. Run over the committed anchors of every column the same tool answers 30 lines at rms 4.39, pitch 75.96, against 17.5 or worse for 31 through 38. The inventory count is superseded by this reading.",
 "form_note": "The right (continuation) sheet of the 1840 schedule. Columns as printed, left to right: twelve SLAVES columns (six male, six female age bands); TOTAL — number of persons in each family; seven NUMBER OF PERSONS IN EACH FAMILY EMPLOYED IN columns (mining, agriculture, commerce, manufactures and trades, navigation of the ocean, navigation of canals lakes and rivers, learned professions and engineers); PENSIONERS FOR REVOLUTIONARY OR MILITARY SERVICES (names, ages); six DEAF AND DUMB, BLIND AND INSANE WHITE PERSONS columns; four of the same for coloured persons; and seven SCHOOLS, &c. columns.",
 "geometry_note": "the leaf is skewed on the exposure: the printed vertical rules drift about +20 px to the RIGHT between y=620 and y=2600, a slope of +0.0101 px per px of y, measured as a rule-position profile in four bands down the page. The reading was made against a sheared copy (x' = x - 0.0101*(y-620), y untouched), on which the rules stand at x = 1258, 1453, 1519, 1583, 1648, 1729, 1809, 1890 and 1955 at every height to within 2 px. Every x quoted in this file is an x on that sheared copy; every y is an y of the exposure itself, because a horizontal shear leaves y alone.",
 "tool_note": "tools/read_census_continuation.py still does not run on this image: 33S7-9YYJ-B1 is one of the four its rule finder exits on with `no industry run bracketed by TOTAL and PENSIONERS`, for the skew recorded above. The reading here was made at magnification against a measured, sheared column map, on the same basis as 33S7-9YYJ-8D. The tool is not weakened to fit.",
 "right_edge_note": "The form's last column — white persons over 20 years of age who cannot read and write — stands at the very edge of the exposure, where the leaf curves into the gutter and goes black. Nothing can be seen written in it on any line, and it is NOT recorded as empty in either direction: the sheet does not say. The seven SCHOOLS, &c. columns to its left are legible and are read.",
 "closure_note": "The enumerator added his own totals at the foot of the sheet. Those totals are transcribed under `footer_as_read` and every column carrying a figure is checked against them in `column_closure`. A column that does not close keeps its residual and its reading is NOT adjusted to make the total come out — the residual is the finding.",
