 "closes": false,
 "closes_note": "TEN of the eleven columns that carry a figure close exactly, and the eleventh is one person out. Agriculture reads 1 against a printed 1; commerce reads 9 against 9; manufactures and trades reads 13 against 13; the learned professions read 2 against 2; the deaf-and-dumb white 25-and-upwards column reads 1 against 1; the primary and common schools read 1 school and 90 scholars against a printed 1 and 90; mining and navigation of the ocean are empty on every line and empty at the foot.\n\nTHE FAMILY TOTALS READ 164 AGAINST A PRINTED 165 — a residual of -1, and nothing was altered to make even that come out. One person on thirty lines is the tightest a continuation sheet has closed in this deposit (33S7-9YYJ-8D is +15, 33S7-9YYJ-5D +3, 33S7-9YYJ-6Q -25), and the reason is the digit key: see `digit_key_used`. Read with the matched pair as 11, as 33S7-9YYJ-8D reads it, this column would be 149 and the residual -16. Where the missing person is cannot be said. The sheet's thirty-first line slot is blank in all 37 columns and was not filled in to absorb it, and no cell was re-read after the sum was known.\n\nNAVIGATION OF CANALS, LAKES AND RIVERS reads 3 against a printed 2, a residual of +1. Three lines carry a mark in it: line 13 at y1581 and line 23 at y2345 are plain slanting strokes of the same weight as the 1s in the manufactures column, and line 22 at y2272 is a stroke whose pen doubles back and leaves a narrow closed loop. If that third mark is not a figure the column closes at 2, and that is the obvious way to make it close — which is exactly why it is not done here. It is committed as a 1 and the residual is left standing.",
 "pairing": {
  "paired_to_printed_page": null,
  "state": "unpaired",
  "why": "a continuation sheet carries no name and no printed page number of its own, so the pairing to its left sheet is a separate reading: the page population below has to be matched against the printed age-band totals at the foot of each candidate left sheet, and those have not been read yet. T-0642 does that for all eleven at once. Recorded as unpaired rather than guessed.",
  "page_population_key": 165,
  "page_population_key_note": "the printed footer figure, which is what a left sheet's age-band sums are to be matched against. The line-by-line reading of the same column is 164; both are published, but they agree to one person, so either will serve the pairing test on this sheet.",
  "line_count_key": 30,
  "schools_key": {
   "primary_common_schools": 1,
   "primary_common_scholars": 90,
   "note": "the only schools figures on the sheet, both on line 1 and both repeated at the foot. A left sheet paired to this one must be able to stand a household on its first line with 90 scholars at one primary or common school — that is a school's roll entered against the family that keeps it, and it is a strong pairing signal."
  },
  "largest_household_key": {
   "line": 27,
   "total": 54,
   "note": "a 54-person household is a strong second signal for the pairing: the left sheet that belongs to this one must carry, on its twenty-seventh line, a name with about fifty-four people distributed across its age bands. On an 1840 Chicago sheet that is a hotel or a boarding house."
  }
 },
